ation. By following the order of operations (multiplication first, then addition), we can accurately calculate the total cost.\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further clarification, feel free to ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the example. It really helps to see how the order of operations applies in a practical scenario.\n\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you found the example helpful. If you have any more questions or need further clarification on the order of operations, arithmetic, or any other topic, feel free to ask. Understanding these concepts is crucial for building a strong foundation in mathematics. If you have any more questions, don't hesitate to ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you provide an example of a real-world problem that involves the order of operations and includes fractions?\n\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Real-world problems often involve calculations that include fractions, and the order of operations must be applied correctly. Let's consider a practical example involving a recipe that requires mixing ingredients in a specific ratio.\n\n### Example Problem:\nSuppose you are making a cake and need to mix ingredients according to the following recipe:\n- For every 2 cups of flour, you need 1 cup of sugar.\n- You have 4 cups of flour and 3 cups of sugar.\n\nYou need to determine the total amount of flour and sugar you will have after mixing them according to the recipe.\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution:\n1. **Determine the amount of sugar needed**:\n   - The recipe requires 1 cup of sugar for every 2 cups of flour.\n   - You have 4 cups of flour.\n   - Calculate the amount of sugar needed:\n     \\[ \\text{Amount of sugar needed} = \\frac{1 \\text{ cup of sugar}}{2 \\text{ cups of flour}} \\times 4 \\text{ cups of flour} = 2 \\text{ cups of sugar} \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the total amount of sugar**:\n   - You already have 3 cups of sugar.\n   - Add the sugar needed from the recipe:\n     \\[ 3 \\text{ cups of sugar} + 2