f product A**:\n   - Number of units: \\( x \\)\n   - Cost per unit: $15\n   - Total cost of product A:\n     \\[ 15x \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the cost of product B**:\n   - Number of units: 2\n   - Cost per unit: $20\n   - Total cost of product B:\n     \\[ 2 \\times 20 = 40 \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the total cost of the inventory**:\n   - Total cost of product A: \\( 15x \\)\n   - Total cost of product B: $40\n   - Total cost of inventory:\n     \\[ 15x + 40 \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the total cost including sales tax**:\n   - Total cost of inventory: \\( 15x + 40 \\)\n   - Sales tax rate: 8% (which can be written as 0.08)\n   - Calculate the sales tax:\n     \\[ \\text{Sales tax} = (15x + 40) \\times 0.08 = 1.2x + 3.2 \\]\n   - Total cost including sales tax:\n     \\[ 15x + 40 + 1.2x + 3.2 = 16.2x + 43.2 \\]\n\n5. **Set up the budget constraint**:\n   - Your budget for the total cost including sales tax is $100.\n   - Set up the equation:\n     \\[ 16.2x + 43.2 = 100 \\]\n\n6. **Solve for the unknown variable \\( x \\)**:\n   - Subtract 43.2 from both sides:\n     \\[ 16.2x = 56.8 \\]\n   - Divide both sides by 16.2:\n     \\[ x = \\frac{56.8}{16.2} \\approx 3.51 \\]\n\n7. **Determine the total cost of the inventory and the shipping fee**:\n   - Total cost of inventory: \\( 15x + 40 \\)\n   - Substitute \\( x \\approx 3.51 \\):\n     \\[ 15 \\times 3.51 + 40 = 52.65 + 40 = 92.65 \\]\n   - Total cost including shipping fee:\n     \\[ 92.65 + 5 = 97.65 \\]\n\n8. **Check if the total cost fits within the budget**:\n   - Total cost including sales tax:\n     \\[ 16.2 \\times 3.51 + 43.2 \\approx 56.8 + 43.2 = 100 \\]\n   - The total cost fits within the budget.\n\n### Summary:\nThis example demonstrates how the order of operations is applied in a real-world problem involving both fractions and percentages, and also requires solving for an unknown variable with multiple steps and conditions, while incorporating a budget constraint. By following the order of operations (multipl